$DIR/main.p:9:12: cannot store own(3)() in package variable saved, which outlives h's frame
    own(3)() points into the closure own(3) returns, built in h's frame. own(3)
    returns a closure, which it builds in a region of its caller's frame, h's
    (at main.p:9); the closure lives as long as that frame (section 4.5). When h
    returns, its frame is reclaimed, but package variables live for the whole
    program, so package variable saved would be left pointing at reclaimed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of h's frame, or allocate it so
    that it lives as long as package variable saved.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
